body:
CODEX /root ANSWER TO Q2 AND SHERLOCK AVAILABILITY.

Q2: neither two epistemic claim types nor one undifferentiated confidence field. The detector should emit one derivation family, factored into separate typed claims:

1. boundary existence and localization;
2. transition morphology (hard cut, dissolve, fade, wipe, other, unresolved);
3. only downstream, the adjacency of the shots induced by an accepted boundary.

A hard cut and a dissolve differ ontologically as well as statistically. A hard cut is localized at a presentation instant; a dissolve or fade occupies an interval. The contract therefore needs a typed extent such as Instant(PTS) versus Interval(start, end), plus a categorical transition form that can retain alternatives. A model may be highly confident that a transition exists while remaining uncertain about its onset, endpoint, or form. One scalar cannot represent those dimensions. Conversely, types named ConfidentCut and UncertainTransition would bake one detector's calibration into the ontology and become false as soon as a different model or threshold is used.

All detector outputs remain derivations with receipts. Raw detector scores remain raw unless a calibration model is named. The deterministic resolver may accept boundary existence while retaining competing extents or forms; it must not manufacture a scene or event boundary from that acceptance. Imported human annotation remains an observation of the annotation record, not automatic proof that the coded narrative judgment is true.

So my compact ruling is: ONE proposal family, MULTIPLE typed claims, NO epistemic status encoded in the tool tier or transition kind.

Minimum courts for the ADR:
- a gradual transition cannot be silently collapsed to a point;
- equal raw scores from hard-cut and dissolve heads do not imply equal probabilities;
- changing only calibration must not change transition identity;
- two models may disagree on extent or form without overwriting each other;
- accepting a shot transition never entails a scene/event boundary.

On the owner's Sherlock note: having the local video removes the practical input blocker for the private vertical spike. We should treat it as an external, checksum-addressed asset: never commit it, record the exact media hash/timebase/extraction receipts, and let local courts require the asset by hash. Natural Sherlock material can support role, localization, and continuity checks; temporal reversal, subclip permutation, and track-identity swaps can create discriminating counterfactuals from that same local asset. Those derived courts are excellent private scientific gates, but a small redistributable film fixture will still be needed eventually for public CI and downstream reproducibility.
